  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1295",
      "html": "<ul><li>Scientific name: Pterygoplichthys punctatus (Kner, 1854)</li><li>Common names: Imperial Ranger Pleco, Guimares Silver Pleco, Punktierter Schilderwels (Germany), Trinidad Pleco, Yogi Pleco</li><li>Type locality: Rio Guaporé, Amazon system, San Vincente, Mato Grosso state, Brazil.</li><li>Synonyms: Glyptoperichthys punctatus, Loricaria punctata, Pterygoplichthys punctatus</li><li>Pronunciation: terry gop LICK thees</li><li>Etymology: Greek, pterygion, diminutive of pteryx = wing, fin + Greek, hoplon = weapon + Greek, ichthys = fish. Lorica, Loricare = cuirass of corslet of leather (a suit of armour made of leather).</li><li>IUCN Red List category: Least Concern. Last assessed 2020.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1295",
      "html": "<p>South America: Amazon basin, including the Middle Amazon (Solimões) and Madeira, the Upper Amazon (Marañón), the Juruá, and the Middle Amazon (Solimões) and Purus.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1295",
      "html": "<p>Temperature: 22.0–26.0°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1295",
      "html": "<p>Omnivorous and a good algae eater, especially when young.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1295",
      "html": "<p>Suggested tankmates: almost any medium sized community fish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1295",
      "html": "<p>Comparison of the genital papilla in mature fish shows the differences in the sexes to the trained eye. In males this is a small yet thick stump which noticeably protrudes from the fish's undercarriage. In females it is less obvious and is recessed or lies flat with the body.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1295",
      "html": "<p>Unreported in captivity. In nature, the male fish occupies a tunnel in soft river bank in which the eggs are laid and guarded. The egg mass of bright orange eggs contains thousands of eggs. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1295",
      "html": "<p>Pterygoplichthys can be identified by the number of rays in the dorsal fin. Ten or more dorsal rays (usually more than 10) indicates that it's a Pterygoplichthys. Most other plecos have 8 or fewer rays (in particular the larger Hypostomus species that are most likely to be confused with Pterygoplichthys).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1295",
      "html": "<p>Denkschriften der Kaiserlichen Akademie der Wissenschaften in Wien, Mathematisch-Naturwissenschaftliche Classe. v. 7, pp 281 [31].</p>"
    }
  ],
